 the campus  arrey bill in georgia is one area where  to a gun control slide but generally  speaking  you've down playing so you're and you're  not seeing the activists left criticize  rob Quist and john Asaf and james  thompson for these stances the way the  heath mellow got hit with abortion so  why is it different you so you can  partially explain that by the geography  obviously they're doing the stances that  makes sense for their localities but  that kind of electoral pragmatism is not  in play with some of these other  subjects would this be allowed on on the  right in the opposite in other words if  you had a republican that checked off  all the right litmus test boxes so to  speak for a reliable republican  candidate except they were very much in  favor of more aggressive gun safety  legislation or whatever the case may be  might the same exception be made on the  other side that seems to be made here by  democrats well you know you generally  know but you susan collins exists yeah  she's been a moderate senator from Maine  for a long time and she doesn't get  denied financial support from the  Republican Party it is standard practice  to allow Running Room to people based on  local concerns like that that's not  unusual and and so in a narrow sense  it's it's no big deal that it's all  running in Montana is going to be  running on a on a gun right stance the  question is is the party prepared to  downplay the issue overall the next time  there is a public mass shooting which we  haven't had in about a year you know we  had a situation where you know after  Bill Clinton passed the Brady Bill and  the assault weapons ban there is an  argument that that hurt Al Gore in 2000  in Arkansas and Tennessee in West  Virginia Democrats began to downplay the  issue generally when the assault weapons  ban came up for renewal in 2004 the  fight for that was very HAP ha R 'td  when you had several pro-gun rights  cats win key Senate races in 2006 Obama  downplayed the is